"""Worked explanations written for a learner following the prerequisite cards."""
SOLUTIONS={
'commitment':{
26:('Perfectly hiding, but not binding.',[
'Write c = m ⊕ r. The published value is c; the message is m; r is a uniformly chosen bit-string randomizer. XOR is applied at each bit position.',
'For a small example, publish c = 1001. The message m = 0011 works with r = 1010, because 0011 ⊕ 1010 = 1001.',
'For hiding, fix any proposed message m and any output c. Exactly one randomizer gives that output: r = c ⊕ m. Since every λ-bit randomizer is equally likely, every c has probability 1/2<sup>λ</sup>, whatever m is. The receiver sees the same distribution for every message.',
'Now test binding separately. Keep c = 1001 but change the message to m′ = 1100. Choose r′ = 0101. The new pair still gives 1001. In general choose r′ = c ⊕ m′.',
'Both different messages pass the opening check. The sender can compute this change efficiently, so even computational binding fails. Perfect hiding did not imply binding.']),
32:('True in the random-oracle model, with sufficiently long randomizers and outputs.',[
'A random oracle assigns independent uniform outputs to distinct inputs and remembers its answers for repeated inputs. Set c = H(m ∥ r), where ∥ joins the message m with a secret fresh randomizer r. Later reveal m and r to open c.',
'If r contains λ random bits, it has 2<sup>λ</sup> possibilities. Testing Q distinct candidates covers at most Q of them, giving the hidden-input guessing bound min(1, Q/2<sup>λ</sup>). For λ = 4 and Q = 2, that is 2/16. These small numbers illustrate the counting, not real security.',
'For binding, the attacker needs two different message openings with the same output. Among Q queried input pairs there are Q(Q−1)/2 pairs to compare. If each output has λ bits, a particular pair collides with probability 1/2<sup>λ</sup>. The union bound is therefore at most min(1, Q(Q−1)/(2·2<sup>λ</sup>)).',
'The collision bound counts all collisions, including some with the same message, so it is still a valid upper bound for the binding attack.',
'A constant success chance needs roughly 2<sup>λ</sup> hidden-input guesses or 2<sup>λ/2</sup> collision-search queries. Both are exponential in λ. Hiding here means learning information about the committed message beyond what was already known; if a message is already known, “recovering” it is not a meaningful security test.']),
33:('False as a claim derived merely from ordinary hash-function security.',[
'First separate the two required properties. Binding stops different openings from passing the same check. Hiding stops the receiver distinguishing which message was committed.',
'Collision resistance helps binding: two different accepted openings would give two different hash inputs with the same output.',
'It does not by itself guarantee hiding. Imagine defining a function whose output contains the first bit of the message, followed by a collision-resistant digest of the full input. Finding a collision in this new function would still require a collision in the digest component.',
'Nevertheless its first output bit openly reveals the first message bit. This is enough to disprove hiding, even if the rest of the digest is hard to attack.',
'The ideal random-oracle proof uses stronger behavior than collision resistance. This argument does not demonstrate an attack on SHA-3; it explains why the stated assumption alone is not a proof of this commitment’s security.']),
53:('Neither statistically hiding nor statistically binding.',[
'The rule is c = H(r)<sup>m</sup>. Here H(r) is a group element, m is an exponent modulo the group order q, and the identity element is written 1.',
'Test message m = 0 first. Every group element to exponent zero equals 1. The output is therefore always 1, regardless of r.',
'For message m = 1, the output is H(r), which is uniform in the group in this model. It equals 1 with probability 1/q. The test “is the output 1?” distinguishes the two messages with probability gap 1−1/q. Hiding fails.',
'Statistical binding must resist unlimited computation. Query two distinct randomizers r₁,r₂. Choose a generator g and compute exponents a,b such that H(r₁)=g<sup>a</sup> and H(r₂)=g<sup>b</sup>. Computing these discrete logarithms is allowed to this unlimited attacker.',
'If a ≠ b, choose message m₁=b with randomizer r₁, and message m₂=a with randomizer r₂. Both give g<sup>ab</sup>, but the messages differ. That is a double opening.',
'The independent uniform exponents a,b coincide with probability 1/q, so the attack succeeds with probability 1−1/q. For example, bases g² and g³ open to the same value using messages 3 and 2. This establishes the statistical-binding failure without claiming the discrete logarithms are efficiently computable.']),
54:('Perfectly hiding, but not binding when the relation between the generators is known.',[
'Write h = g<sup>x</sup>, with known nonzero x. The commitment becomes c = g<sup>m</sup>h<sup>r</sup> = g<sup>m+xr</sup>. All exponent calculations below are modulo q.',
'Why hiding remains: multiplication by nonzero x rearranges the q possible remainders because x has an inverse. Adding a fixed m also rearranges them. Therefore uniform r makes m+xr uniform, independently of the chosen message.',
'To change the message to m′ while keeping c, keep its exponent equal: m+xr ≡ m′+xr′. Subtract m′, then multiply by the inverse of x. This gives r′ = r+(m−m′)x⁻¹ modulo q.',
'Example: q=11, x=3, m=2, r=3. The exponent is 2+3·3=11, or remainder 0. Since 3·4≡1 modulo 11, x⁻¹=4. Choose m′=7; then r′=3+(2−7)·4≡5. The new exponent 7+3·5=22 also has remainder 0.',
'Both openings produce the same group element. The arithmetic is efficient when x is known, so binding fails. Real Pedersen setup must keep that relation hard for the sender to find.']),
55:('Perfectly binding, but not statistically hiding.',[
'Call the published pair (a,b) = (g<sup>r</sup>, g<sup>m</sup>h<sup>r</sup>). Suppose two openings (m,r) and (m′,r′) produce that identical pair.',
'The first coordinates agree: g<sup>r</sup>=g<sup>r′</sup>. Powers of a generator repeat exactly modulo the group order q, so r=r′ as allowed remainders.',
'Cancel the now-equal mask h<sup>r</sup> in the second coordinates. We get g<sup>m</sup>=g<sup>m′</sup>, hence m=m′ modulo q. Distinct allowed messages cannot open the same pair. This is perfect binding, even against unlimited computation.',
'For hiding, use the known x with h=g<sup>x</sup>. Compute b/a<sup>x</sup>. Since a<sup>x</sup>=g<sup>rx</sup>=h<sup>r</sup>, this cancels the mask and leaves g<sup>m</sup>. Division here uses a group inverse.',
'An unlimited observer can recover m by a discrete logarithm. Moreover an efficient observer given two candidate messages can compare their powers to g<sup>m</sup>. Thus the scheme is not statistically hiding; with known x it also fails the usual message-distinguishing version of computational hiding.']),
60:('False: secure PRFs are incompatible with P = NP.',[
'A PRF uses one secret key k to define every answer F(k,x). A genuinely random function instead chooses independent answers at different inputs and remembers them.',
'Collect answers y₁,…,yₜ at distinct inputs x₁,…,xₜ. Ask the yes/no question: “Does some key k satisfy F(k,xᵢ)=yᵢ for every observed pair?” A proposed key can be checked by evaluating F on the listed inputs. It is an efficiently checkable witness, so this is an NP question.',
'If P=NP, this existence question can be decided efficiently. No unexplained key-recovery step is needed: the yes/no answer itself will distinguish the experiments.',
'In the real-PRF experiment the answer is always yes, because the actual key fits. In the random-function experiment, suppose keys have κ bits and outputs have ℓ bits. A fixed key matches t independent random answers with probability 2<sup>−ℓt</sup>. There are at most 2<sup>κ</sup> keys, so the union bound gives at most 2<sup>κ−ℓt</sup> for any key fitting.',
'Choose polynomially many distinct inputs so ℓt exceeds κ by the desired security margin, as available in the standard PRF setting. Then the random-function experiment almost always answers no, while the PRF experiment always answers yes. An efficient distinguisher contradicts PRF security.']),
62:('False if the statement requires identical ciphertexts.',[
'Enc(m) means encrypt message m, producing a ciphertext c. Dec(c) means decrypt it. Eval(f,c) computes a new encrypted representation intended to represent f(m).',
'Encryption normally uses randomness. Two encryptions of the same message can therefore have different bytes while decrypting to the same value.',
'Write the randomness explicitly: c₁ = Enc(pk,3;r₁) and c₂ = Enc(pk,3;r₂), where pk is the public encryption key. Different random choices r₁ and r₂ may produce c₁ ≠ c₂ while both decrypt to 3.',
'The correctness requirement is Dec(Eval(f,c)) = f(m), for a valid encryption c of m and functions supported by the scheme. If m=3 and f doubles its input, the evaluated ciphertext must decrypt to 6.',
'It need not equal a newly sampled Enc(6) byte for byte. The missing distinction in the proposed equality is between the encrypted representation and its decrypted content.']),
72:('The proposed simulation is not the random-oracle experiment, so the claimed advantage transfer is unjustified.',[
'The simulation answers every distinct hash query with the same value X. A true random oracle instead samples an independent uniform group value at each new input, and only repeats an answer when the input repeats.',
'Ask two different inputs m₁,m₂ and compare the outputs. In the proposed simulation, H(m₁)=H(m₂) with probability 1.',
'In a random oracle with q possible outputs, the second independent output matches the first with probability 1/q. The gap is 1−1/q, which is easy to detect.',
'An attacker that works well against the genuine PRF game may behave completely differently on this detectable fake game. Therefore its PRF advantage does not automatically become a DDH advantage.',
'This does not claim every possible attacker has DDH advantage zero. It says the proposed simulation gives no general justification for the promised reduction. Correct the distribution before using the attacker’s assumed guarantee.']),
73:('Shift both relevant exponents using the same fresh random number.',[
'Let X=g<sup>α</sup>, Y=g<sup>β</sup>, Z=g<sup>γ</sup>. We do not need to know those exponents to perform the group operations. Pick a uniform r in Z_q, then set X′=Xg<sup>r</sup> and Z′=ZY<sup>r</sup>. Keep Y unchanged.',
'The exponent of X′ is α+r. Adding a fixed value to all possible remainders rearranges them, so uniform r makes X′ uniform.',
'In the real case γ=αβ. The new third exponent is αβ+βr=(α+r)β. Thus Z′=(X′)<sup>β</sup>, exactly the real relation for the new first element.',
'In the random case γ is independent and uniform. Conditional on r, adding βr to γ preserves uniformity. Thus Z′ is uniform and independent of X′ and Y in the random-challenge experiment.',
'Example in exponent arithmetic modulo 5: α=1, β=2, r=3. Then α′=4 and the real γ′=2+2·3≡3. Also α′β=4·2≡3. This numerical check illustrates the general algebra; it is not a security proof by itself.']),
75:('Use a separate stored shift for each distinct input; one PRF answer then has the exact required distribution.',[
'Maintain a table indexed by the queried message. The first time input mᵢ appears, choose a fresh uniform rᵢ and store H(mᵢ)=Xg<sup>rᵢ</sup>. If the input repeats, return that stored value. If the PRF input appears first, create its table entry then.',
'Each fresh shift rearranges all exponents, so each new hash answer is uniform. The shifts are independent, so distinct hash inputs receive independent answers. The table ensures repeated inputs remain consistent.',
'For the one allowed PRF query mⱼ, return ZY<sup>rⱼ</sup>. In the real DDH world Z=g<sup>αβ</sup>, so this equals g<sup>(α+rⱼ)β</sup>=H(mⱼ)<sup>β</sup>. It is the genuine PRF with key β.',
'In the random DDH world, Z is an independent uniform group element. Multiplying it by Y<sup>rⱼ</sup> keeps this single response uniform even given the hash answers. It therefore matches the one answer of a genuinely random function.',
'The two distributions match exactly for this single-query setting. The reduction no longer guesses which hash query will later matter, so there is no query-index probability loss. Multiple PRF answers need the separate analysis in the next exercise.']),
77:('Use two independent random numbers per input so that both output exponents can vary independently.',[
'For each new message mᵢ choose independent uniform uᵢ,vᵢ in Z_q. Store H(mᵢ)=X<sup>uᵢ</sup>g<sup>vᵢ</sup> and f(mᵢ)=Z<sup>uᵢ</sup>Y<sup>vᵢ</sup>. Return stored values on repeats. The symbols u and v here name random exponents, not protocol messages.',
'In the real DDH case, the exponent of the first value is aᵢ=αuᵢ+vᵢ. The second exponent is αβuᵢ+βvᵢ=βaᵢ. Hence every answer satisfies f(mᵢ)=H(mᵢ)<sup>β</sup>, all under one key β.',
'In the random case, the exponent pair is aᵢ=αuᵢ+vᵢ and bᵢ=γuᵢ+βvᵢ. The map from (uᵢ,vᵢ) to (aᵢ,bᵢ) has matrix [[α,1],[γ,β]] over Z_q and determinant αβ−γ. A matrix with nonzero determinant over a field is invertible, so every output pair has exactly one input pair.',
'Therefore independent uniform input pairs give independent uniform output pairs. Fresh uᵢ,vᵢ for distinct messages also make those pairs independent across messages. This is the joint distribution that a random oracle and independent random function require.',
'The exceptional event γ=αβ occurs with probability 1/q when γ is uniformly random. On that event the output exponents are related, so we cannot claim exact random-world equality. Allowing this event gives advantage loss at most 1/q, rather than a multiplicative loss for guessing one query.',
'Invertibility is what makes the simulation work: every pair is produced equally often, so the answers have the joint distribution required by the random experiment.']),
81:('Publish one hash value per position.',[
'For vector (m₁,…,mₙ), publish c=(H(m₁),…,H(mₙ)). Each hash has λ bits, so n of them use nλ bits. The index determines which coordinate is being claimed.',
'To open index i, supply the message mᵢ. The verifier computes H(mᵢ) and compares it with the stored coordinate cᵢ. No additional hash path or other proof data is required: the extra proof π is empty.',
'The problem counts the size of π separately from the claimed index and message. Thus π has constant size O(1); this does not mean communicating the index and the message costs zero bits.',
'If two different messages both verify at the same index, their hashes both equal cᵢ. They are distinct inputs with the same hash output: a collision. The random-oracle collision bound makes finding this unlikely with polynomially many queries.',
'Example: publishing eight 256-bit hashes uses 2048 bits. Opening one entry adds no extra authentication hashes, but you still send the entry you want checked.']),
82:('Publish one hash of the complete, unambiguously encoded vector.',[
'Encode the ordered messages so their boundaries are clear, then publish c=H(encode(m₁,…,mₙ)). For fixed-length messages simple concatenation works; for variable lengths include lengths or another unambiguous encoding.',
'One hash output uses λ bits, whatever the vector length. That makes the commitment short.',
'To check a particular index, the straightforward verifier needs the full vector. Supply all messages as the opening information, recompute the complete digest, and check both that digest and the claimed index value.',
'If each message has |m| bits, this opening contains O(n|m|) bits. The claimed value can be omitted from the auxiliary proof if the verifier already receives it separately; the asymptotic bound is unchanged.',
'If different values at the same index both verify, the two complete encodings differ but have the same hash. That gives a collision. The construction trades a short commitment for a long opening; it does not hide the other messages when opened.']),
84:('Use a balanced binary Merkle tree and reveal one sibling hash per level.',[
'Hash each message to form a leaf. Hash every ordered pair of neighboring child hashes to form a parent. Continue until the root, and publish that one λ-bit value. Fix the tree shape, index range and leaf/internal-node encoding so verification is unambiguous.',
'To open one leaf, the verifier starts with the hash of the claimed message. At each level it needs the other child’s hash: the sibling. The index tells whether the current value belongs on the left or right.',
'For leaf 4 of 8, first combine leaf hash 3 on the left with leaf hash 4 on the right. Then combine the hash of leaves 1–2 on the left. Finally combine the hash of leaves 5–8 on the right. These three sibling values rebuild the root.',
'A balanced tree of n leaves has O(log₂ n) levels, so the proof has O(log n · λ) bits of sibling hashes, plus any needed index/shape information. For n=8 the sibling portion is exactly three hash values.',
'For binding, compare two accepted openings for different messages at the same index. If their initial leaf hashes already agree, they give a collision. Otherwise trace upward: the roots agree, so there is a first point where different child inputs give the same parent hash. That too is a collision.',
'This gives both a short commitment and a short opening. Left/right order is essential: the same hashes in the wrong order need not reconstruct the root.'])},
'zero-knowledge':{
16:('True under the hypothetical assumption P = NP.',[
'An NP language has efficiently checkable evidence for each yes-instance. If P=NP, there is also an efficient algorithm that decides yes or no directly from the public input.',
'Let the verifier run that decision algorithm. The prover sends nothing, so no secret witness needs to be supplied.',
'For a true statement the verifier accepts, giving completeness. For a false one it rejects regardless of what a cheating prover wants, giving soundness.',
'The verifier receives an empty transcript. A simulator can output the same empty transcript without a witness. Thus the protocol is zero knowledge.',
'This is a conditional argument: it does not assert that P=NP is known to be true.']),
17:('False: perfect completeness assumes an honest verifier.',[
'Perfect completeness says that an honest prover and an honest verifier accept a true statement with probability 1.',
'The statement in the question changes the verifier to a malicious one. That means its program may deviate from the prescribed verification rule.',
'Choose the malicious program “ignore every message and reject”. It rejects true statements too, so it is a direct counterexample to “will always accept”.',
'This does not break the definition: the definition never promised acceptance by arbitrary dishonest verifiers. Soundness error 1/3 is about cheating provers on false statements and does not change this counterexample.']),
18:('False: a malicious prover may deliberately reveal its witness.',[
'The zero-knowledge promise describes information obtained from the prescribed honest prover. It does not force every possible program playing the prover role to keep secrets.',
'Consider a malicious prover that sends its full witness as an extra message. For a circuit-satisfiability statement, it could send the entire satisfying assignment in plain text.',
'The honest verifier can now learn that witness. There is no contradiction: the prover voluntarily departed from the behavior protected by the privacy guarantee.',
'Keep the quantifiers separate: zero knowledge protects the honest prover against the specified class of verifiers; soundness protects an honest verifier against cheating provers.']),
54:('True: for a language in P, the verifier can decide the claim itself.',[
'P means membership has an efficient yes/no decision algorithm. The public statement already contains the input to that algorithm.',
'Use a protocol in which the prover sends no message. The verifier runs the algorithm and accepts exactly the yes-instances.',
'That gives completeness on true statements and soundness on false statements. There is no interaction, so the protocol is non-interactive.',
'A simulator reproduces the empty transcript without a witness. This is zero knowledge in the ordinary model without any ideal random oracle.',
'The restriction “languages in P” is essential. This simple construction does not decide arbitrary NP statements unless the additional hypothesis P=NP holds.']),
39:('Sequential repetition lowers soundness error and supports sequential simulation under the stated ZK guarantee.',[
'One graph-protocol round has cheating success at most 1/2. In k sequential rounds, the verifier accepts only if every round accepts.',
'Use conditional probabilities: the first success has probability at most 1/2; given the first success, the next still has probability at most 1/2 under the per-round soundness condition. Continuing gives at most (1/2)<sup>k</sup>. For k=4, that is 1/16.',
'For privacy, simulate the first round against the verifier. Retain the verifier’s resulting state, including earlier messages and its stored information.',
'Then simulate the next round with that state as the starting information, and continue. Finishing one round before beginning the next lets each simulator handle one interaction at a time.',
'This relies on a single-round zero-knowledge guarantee that applies with the verifier’s carried state, as in the appropriate sequential-composition theorem. Repeating a procedure does not by itself prove that theorem; the state requirement is the reason the sequential argument is stated carefully.']),
41:('The single-round simulation argument does not automatically establish malicious-verifier ZK in parallel.',[
'In parallel execution all first messages are sent before any challenge response is completed. A malicious verifier may compute the whole challenge vector jointly from those messages.',
'The graph-protocol simulator prepares a commitment that it can open for one anticipated challenge. For one fair challenge bit, a guess succeeds with probability 1/2.',
'If that method must guess a whole k-bit challenge vector at once, there are 2<sup>k</sup> possibilities. Its success can be as low as 2<sup>−k</sup>, with expected retry cost 2<sup>k</sup>. That is exponential, not an efficient simulation.',
'We therefore cannot reuse this sequential simulator to conclude general ZK for the parallel protocol. The failure is a gap in that proof method, not a proof that every parallel protocol necessarily leaks information.',
'The next part imposes an honest verifier, whose independent challenge choices allow a different, direct simulation.']),
42:('Honest-verifier zero knowledge is preserved by independently simulating the copies.',[
'For one copy, an honest-verifier simulator produces a transcript (a,e,z) indistinguishable from a real honest execution, without knowing a witness. For perfect HVZK the distributions agree exactly; for computational HVZK efficient tests cannot distinguish them.',
'Run that simulator independently k times, obtaining (a₁,e₁,z₁), …, (aₖ,eₖ,zₖ). Independent runs mean using fresh random choices each time.',
'Arrange the results in the parallel order: the batch of first messages, the batch of challenges, and the batch of responses.',
'The honest parallel verifier samples the challenges independently according to the protocol. The independently simulated copies match the honest joint distribution in the perfect case. In the computational case, replace one real copy by its simulated version at a time. Each change is indistinguishable; polynomially many such changes preserve that guarantee.',
'This conclusion is HVZK: it is specifically about the honest verifier. A malicious verifier choosing correlated challenges is outside this argument.']),
44:('Perfect completeness follows by substituting the honest response.',[
'The public values are g and h=g<sup>x</sup>. The prover knows secret exponent x, chooses random r, and sends u=g<sup>r</sup>. The verifier chooses challenge c. The response is z=r+cx modulo q.',
'The verifier checks g<sup>z</sup>=u·h<sup>c</sup>. Substitute the definition of z: g<sup>z</sup>=g<sup>r+cx</sup>. Exponents may be reduced modulo q because q is the group order.',
'Use the power rules: g<sup>r+cx</sup>=g<sup>r</sup>(g<sup>x</sup>)<sup>c</sup>. Replace g<sup>r</sup> by the sent u and g<sup>x</sup> by the public h. The result is exactly u·h<sup>c</sup>.',
'This identity holds for every honest r and c, not just likely ones. Therefore acceptance probability is 1.',
'Numerical check in the order-11 subgroup modulo 23: g=2, x=4, h=16, r=3 gives u=8. For c=2, z=3+2·4≡0 modulo 11. The two sides are 2⁰=1 and 8·16²≡1 modulo 23.']),
45:('Choose the normally later values first, then solve for the first message.',[
'The transcript is (u,c,z), and it must satisfy g<sup>z</sup>=u·h<sup>c</sup>. The simulator knows public g,h,q but does not know secret x.',
'Choose independent uniform c,z in Z_q. Rearrange the public checking equation to obtain u=g<sup>z</sup>h<sup>−c</sup>. Negative exponent means use the group inverse. Output (u,c,z).',
'The equation verifies automatically: multiplying the computed u by h<sup>c</sup> cancels h<sup>−c</sup>, leaving g<sup>z</sup>. This establishes acceptance, but distribution matching still needs a separate argument.',
'In a real honest run, c is uniform. For any fixed c, adding the fixed cx to uniform r rearranges Z_q, so z=r+cx is uniform too. Thus the real pair (c,z) is independent uniform, exactly as sampled by the simulator.',
'Given c,z, the checking equation uniquely determines u. The finished triples therefore have the same joint distribution. This is perfect honest-verifier zero knowledge, not merely a procedure for making one accepting-looking transcript.']),
46:('Two different accepted challenges with the same first message reveal the witness.',[
'Run the prover until it sends u, and save its program state. Send a uniform challenge c and obtain an accepted z. Restore that saved state and send an independent uniform c′, obtaining accepted z′. The question assumes the prover can answer every challenge.',
'The accepted equations are g<sup>z</sup>=u h<sup>c</sup> and g<sup>z′</sup>=u h<sup>c′</sup>. Divide them. Because u is identical in both, it cancels: g<sup>z−z′</sup>=h<sup>c−c′</sup>.',
'Write h=g<sup>x</sup>. Equal powers of a generator give z−z′≡x(c−c′) modulo q. If c≠c′, the nonzero difference has an inverse since q is prime. Hence x=(z−z′)(c−c′)⁻¹ modulo q.',
'Example modulo 11: use (c,z)=(2,0) and (c′,z′)=(5,1) with the same u. The differences are 10 and 8. Since 8·7≡1, the extracted x is 10·7≡4.',
'Two independent uniform challenges coincide with probability 1/q. That is the failure event for this extraction attempt, so the stated success probability is 1−1/q under the always-answering assumption. Changing u between runs would prevent the cancellation.']),
57:('Commit to shared wire bits, prove every gate rule, and check the final output.',[
'Begin with a satisfying assignment: input bits that make the circuit output 1. Evaluate the gates to obtain every intermediate wire bit. Let m_w name the bit on wire w.',
'For each wire choose fresh randomness r_w and publish c_w=Commit(m_w,r_w). Reuse that exact commitment at every occurrence of the same wire. Otherwise neighboring gates could refer to different bits.',
'For every gate start the given XOR or AND Sigma proof on its three wire commitments. Send all wire commitments and all first proof messages together. The subprotocols are supplied by the exercise; we are describing how to connect them.',
'The honest verifier sends independent random challenges for the gate proofs. The prover returns the corresponding responses and opens the final output wire to 1.',
'The verifier checks every local proof, checks that the final opening is valid, and checks that its bit is 1. Accept only if all checks pass.',
'The circuit example t=a XOR b, out=t AND a shows why one shared t matters: both gates must use the same commitment for t. Checking only the output or only isolated gates would miss this consistency requirement.']),
58:('An accepted false circuit must contain an accepted false local gate statement.',[
'Assume the circuit is unsatisfiable, meaning no input assignment makes its output 1. Suppose a cheating prover nevertheless gets the verifier to accept.',
'Perfect binding means each wire commitment has at most one possible bit opening. The accepted final opening fixes the output bit to 1. A commitment with no valid opening cannot make an incident gate statement true either.',
'If every gate statement were true, their shared commitments would define compatible bits satisfying every gate rule. Following these wires would be a valid circuit evaluation ending at 1. That contradicts unsatisfiability.',
'Therefore at least one gate statement must be false, yet its local verifier accepted. This identifies which security property has to fail: soundness of a component gate proof.',
'If total cheating succeeds with probability ε and there are N gates, the probability of some false gate acceptance is at most the sum over gates. A sum of N numbers that is at least ε contains a number at least ε/N. For example, if ε=0.1 and N=10, some term is at least 0.01.',
'For polynomial N, dividing a non-negligible probability by N keeps it non-negligible. The reduction uses the accepted false local statement to contradict the corresponding component soundness guarantee.']),
61:('The suggested simulator explains the intended idea, but an extra guarantee is needed to make the final step rigorous.',[
'The simulator must reproduce wire commitments, all gate-proof transcripts, and the final opening without knowing a satisfying assignment. Hiding should conceal the wire values; gate-proof simulation should imitate the messages.',
'The informal proposal commits to bit 1 on every wire using fresh randomness. It can then open the final output as 1. For each gate it proposes calling the corresponding simulator to make an accepting-looking transcript.',
'The intended comparison changes real transcripts to simulated ones and real wire commitments to these fake commitments. A chain changing one component at a time is called a hybrid argument. Each neighboring change must be justified by a security guarantee.',
'Here is the difficulty: setting all wire bits to 1 makes an XOR gate false, because 1⊕1=0, not 1. Ordinary HVZK promises simulation on true public statements. It does not automatically promise an accepting transcript for this false statement.',
'So the stated ingredients alone do not justify simply calling that simulator on all fake gates. A complete proof must establish stronger simulator behavior for these specific subprotocols or provide a different valid hybrid construction.',
'The appropriate beginner lesson is to identify the missing step rather than memorize a claim stronger than the assumptions support. The homework requests an informal argument; the plan above gives that intention while making its precise limitation visible.'])}}
